# Folkprotocol Source Maps v0
#
# First experimental slice of a replacement content flow. It commissions a
# bounded question, runs explicit source-domain passes, and stops with maps.
# It does not select practices, compare domains, design protocols, or draft a
# page.
#
# Usage after lodging in ~/.mill/chains:
#   mill crank folkprotocol-source-maps-v0 \
#     slug=hangover \
#     question="What might reduce or prevent an alcohol hangover?" \
#     boundaries="Adults; prevention and next-day symptoms; no withdrawal care" \
#     seeds="Existing Folkprotocol hangover page and bibliography" \
#     --attach

Expect slug
Expect question
Default boundaries: none supplied
Default seeds: none supplied
Default work_dir: /home/patrick/projects/folkprotocol

Work in {work_dir}

# Stage 1: preserve the research commission and create a durable place for
# editorial impetus. The commission is frozen before research; 01 may later be
# amended without invalidating the independent source maps.
CC: """Create research/source-maps/{slug}/00-commission.md and
research/source-maps/{slug}/01-editorial-direction.md for this Folkprotocol
investigation.

If 00-commission.md already exists and its final nonblank line is `COMMISSION
COMPLETE`, leave it unchanged. If 01-editorial-direction.md also exists and its
final nonblank line is `EDITORIAL DIRECTION COMPLETE`, leave it unchanged. Do
not stop until both files have been checked; create only whichever is absent or
incomplete.

Record exactly:
- slug: {slug}
- question: {question}
- boundaries: {boundaries}
- seeds supplied by the commissioner: {seeds}

Add a short section called Working boundary which explains the supplied scope
without adding adjacent goals. Add a section called Not decided yet stating
that no practice, protocol, page thesis, or source hierarchy has been chosen.

In 01-editorial-direction.md record the slug and an `Initial impetus` section
containing the supplied seeds without upgrading them into factual claims. Add
`Later direction` containing `None supplied yet.` This file is the durable hook
for commissioner observations or priorities supplied after mapping begins; it
is not a source map or evidence record. End it with `EDITORIAL DIRECTION
COMPLETE`.

Do not research the topic in this step. Do not recommend anything. Do not
silently widen the commission. Create the directory if needed and write only
these two files. End 00-commission.md with the exact final line `COMMISSION
COMPLETE`.""" timeout=300

# Stage 2: map deliberately seeded source domains. Each pass writes its own
# file. These are maps of a knowledge territory, not lists of remedies and not
# proto-protocols.
Parallel(4):
    CC: """Map the Reddit and general-forum territory around this question:
{question}

Commission boundary: {boundaries}
Commission seeds: {seeds}

If research/source-maps/{slug}/10-reddit-forums.md already exists and its final
nonblank line begins `MAP COMPLETE —`, run `wc -w` on it. If it is at most
2,500 words, leave it unchanged and stop. If it is longer, do not redo the
research: shorten the existing map without removing the marker until it is at
most 2,500 words, then stop.

Use web search and direct page access. Do not use browser-harness. Look across
more than one relevant community when the topic permits it. Search for useful
vocabulary, recurring ways people frame the problem, recognizable schools of
thought, preparation or timing patterns, disagreements, negative reports,
follow-ups, and community-specific assumptions. Describe recurring practices
only at map scale; do not turn every mention into a protocol candidate.

Work on this pass yourself. Do not delegate, spawn subagents, or launch another
Mill, Spindle, Horizon, or agent workflow.

Evaluate community material as community material. Do not compare it with
clinical research, and do not treat repetition as proof. Notice likely
copying, influencer or vendor effects, deleted context, selection effects, and
whether posters return with outcomes.

Bound the pass: aim for 8–12 useful sources, with 12 an absolute maximum rather
than a quota. Stop earlier when additional search routes are only repeating the
same vocabulary and strains of thought. Never fill a gap from memory. Keep the
entire map at most 2,500 words: no more than six broad strains of thought, six
meaningful disagreements or gaps, and a concise source index.

Write research/source-maps/{slug}/10-reddit-forums.md with:
1. Search routes and communities visited
2. Vocabulary and conceptual terrain
3. Recurring strains of thought, kept broad
4. Disagreements, negative space, and visible failures
5. Access failures and unanswered questions
6. Source index

Write only this file; create no other files.
Use local citation IDs [RF1], [RF2], and so on in the map. Every substantive
description must point to a source. For each source record title, author or
handle when public, community/site, date, stable URL, access date, the relevant
post/comment context, and what it contributed. Clearly label researcher
inference rather than presenting it as a source claim. Mark any source not
opened directly as snippet-only, and preserve one short verbatim excerpt from
each source, no longer than 35 words, so later verification does not depend on
the page remaining live. One citation ID must identify one retrievable page or
thread; never bundle several sources under one ID. Every ID used in the body
must have exactly one source-index entry, and every source-index entry must be
cited at least once in the body. The N in the marker counts source-index
entries.
Do not recommend, compare domains, select practices, or design a test. End the
file with `MAP COMPLETE — N sources used`, replacing N with the source count.
Then run `wc -w research/source-maps/{slug}/10-reddit-forums.md`. If it exceeds
2,500 words, shorten the map without removing the marker and repeat the check
until the final file is at most 2,500 words.""" timeout=2400

    CC: """Map traditional lineages and inherited practice around this question:
{question}

Commission boundary: {boundaries}
Commission seeds: {seeds}

If research/source-maps/{slug}/20-traditional.md already exists and its final
nonblank line begins `MAP COMPLETE —`, run `wc -w` on it. If it is at most
2,500 words, leave it unchanged and stop. If it is longer, do not redo the
research: shorten the existing map without removing the marker until it is at
most 2,500 words, then stop.

Search across relevant named traditions rather than treating 'traditional use'
as one bucket. Preserve each tradition's own categories, indications,
preparations, sequencing, combinations, and internal disagreements. Note when
modern English summaries appear to flatten or mistranslate the underlying
pathway. Describe recurring practices only at map scale; do not extract an
endless remedy list or force traditions into modern diagnostic categories.

Work on this pass yourself. Do not delegate, spawn subagents, or launch another
Mill, Spindle, Horizon, or agent workflow.

Do not use formal clinical research as the judge of this domain. Also do not
turn age, sacred status, or repetition into automatic truth. The task is to
make the traditional terrain legible in context.

Bound the pass: aim for 8–12 useful sources, with 12 an absolute maximum rather
than a quota, favoring identifiable texts, lineages, archives, pharmacopoeias,
or careful secondary sources. Stop earlier at clear saturation. Never invent
inaccessible quotations or page references. Keep the entire map at most 2,500
words: no more than six broad pathways, six meaningful variations or gaps, and
a concise source index.

Write research/source-maps/{slug}/20-traditional.md with:
1. Traditions and search routes visited
2. Native vocabulary and conceptual terrain
3. Major pathways or strains of thought, kept intact and broad
4. Variations, disagreements, and negative space
5. Translation problems, access failures, and unanswered questions
6. Source index

Write only this file; create no other files.
Use local citation IDs [TR1], [TR2], and so on. Every substantive description
must point to a source. Record title, author/editor, tradition, date, edition or
page/section when available, URL or archive location, access date, and what the
source contributed. Clearly distinguish quotation, paraphrase, and researcher
inference. Mark any source not opened directly as snippet-only. Do not
recommend, compare domains, select practices, or design a test. One citation ID
must identify one retrievable text, page, or document; never bundle several
sources under one ID. Every ID used in the body must have exactly one
source-index entry, and every source-index entry must be cited at least once in
the body. The N in the marker counts source-index entries. End the file
with `MAP COMPLETE — N sources used`, replacing N with the source count. Then
run `wc -w research/source-maps/{slug}/20-traditional.md`. If it exceeds 2,500
words, shorten the map without removing the marker and repeat the check until
the final file is at most 2,500 words.""" timeout=2400

    CC: """Map the formal published-research territory around this question:
{question}

Commission boundary: {boundaries}
Commission seeds: {seeds}

If research/source-maps/{slug}/30-published-research.md already exists and its
final nonblank line begins `MAP COMPLETE —`, run `wc -w` on it. If it is at
most 2,500 words, leave it unchanged and stop. If it is longer, do not redo the
research: shorten the existing map without removing the marker until it is at
most 2,500 words, then stop.

Search PubMed and other relevant scholarly indexes, including work outside
Western medicine when discoverable. Map the questions researchers have asked,
the preparations and populations actually studied, recurring mechanisms or
models, null and adverse findings, review articles, and obvious blind spots.
Do not reduce the map to an evidence ranking or a list of favorable trials.
A study is one source situated in this domain, not the court of appeal for the
other maps. Treat weak, irrelevant, or badly translated studies accordingly.

Work on this pass yourself. Do not delegate, spawn subagents, or launch another
Mill, Spindle, Horizon, or agent workflow.

Bound the pass: aim for 8–12 useful sources, with 12 an absolute maximum rather
than a quota, and stop earlier when new searches only repeat the same research
programs. Prefer primary sources for specific study details; reviews may map
the field but must not be misnamed as trials or meta-analyses. Never infer
full-text details from an abstract or search snippet alone. Keep the entire map
at most 2,500 words: no more than six broad research programs, six meaningful
disputes or gaps, and a concise source index.

Write research/source-maps/{slug}/30-published-research.md with:
1. Indexes and search routes used
2. Vocabulary and research terrain
3. Major research programs or strains of thought
4. Null results, disputes, and neglected questions
5. Material, preparation, population, or outcome mismatches
6. Access failures and unanswered questions
7. Source index

Write only this file; create no other files.
Use local citation IDs [PR1], [PR2], and so on. Every substantive description
must point to a source. Record full citation, DOI/PMID or stable URL, access
date, source type, what was actually studied, and what the source contributes
to the map. Mark abstract-only access. Clearly label researcher inference. Do
not recommend, compare domains, select practices, or design a test. One
citation ID must identify one retrievable paper or document; never bundle
several sources under one ID. Every ID used in the body must have exactly one
source-index entry, and every source-index entry must be cited at least once in
the body. The N in the marker counts source-index entries. End the
file with `MAP COMPLETE — N sources used`, replacing N with the source count.
Then run `wc -w research/source-maps/{slug}/30-published-research.md`. If it
exceeds 2,500 words, shorten the map without removing the marker and repeat the
check until the final file is at most 2,500 words.""" timeout=2400

    CC: """Map the commercial web territory around this question:
{question}

Commission boundary: {boundaries}
Commission seeds: {seeds}

If research/source-maps/{slug}/40-commercial-web.md already exists and its
final nonblank line begins `MAP COMPLETE —`, run `wc -w` on it. If it is at
most 2,500 words, leave it unchanged and stop. If it is longer, do not redo the
research: shorten the existing map without removing the marker until it is at
most 2,500 words, then stop.

Search product pages, supplement sellers, clinics, affiliate sites, marketing
copy, patents when relevant, and visible product-review ecosystems. Map what is
being sold, recurring formulations and doses, the stories used to justify
them, market convergence, disagreement, and claims that appear copied across
vendors. Treat this material neither as worthless nor as validation: it shows
what products and narratives people actually encounter.

Work on this pass yourself. Do not delegate, spawn subagents, or launch another
Mill, Spindle, Horizon, or agent workflow.

Bound the pass: aim for 8–12 useful sources across multiple owners, with 12 an
absolute maximum rather than a quota. Do not let many storefronts repeating
manufacturer copy masquerade as independent convergence. Stop when additional
results add no new formulation or narrative. Keep the entire map at most 2,500
words: no more than six broad formulation or narrative families, six
meaningful disagreements or gaps, and a concise source index.

Write research/source-maps/{slug}/40-commercial-web.md with:
1. Search routes and market segments visited
2. Product and claim vocabulary
3. Recurring formulation and narrative families
4. Disagreements, customer-reported failures, and negative space
5. Copying, ownership, affiliate, and incentive notes
6. Access failures and unanswered questions
7. Source index

Write only this file; create no other files.
Use local citation IDs [CW1], [CW2], and so on. Every substantive description
must point to a source. Record company/site, page or product title, formulation
when visible, date when visible, stable URL, access date, ownership or affiliate
relationship when discoverable, and what it contributed. Separate vendor
claims, customer reports, and researcher inference. Do not recommend, compare
domains, select practices, or design a test. Mark any source not opened
directly as snippet-only, and preserve one short verbatim excerpt per source so
later verification does not depend on the page remaining live; each excerpt
must be no longer than 35 words. One citation ID must identify one retrievable
page or document; never bundle several sources under one ID. Every ID used in
the body must have exactly one source-index entry, and every source-index entry
must be cited at least once in the body. The N in the marker counts
source-index entries. End the file
with `MAP COMPLETE — N sources used`, replacing N with the source count. Then
run `wc -w research/source-maps/{slug}/40-commercial-web.md`. If it exceeds
2,500 words, shorten the map without removing the marker and repeat the check
until the final file is at most 2,500 words.""" timeout=2400

    CC: """Map experimental self-tracking communities around this question:
{question}

Commission boundary: {boundaries}
Commission seeds: {seeds}

If research/source-maps/{slug}/50-experimental-communities.md already exists
and its final nonblank line begins `MAP COMPLETE —`, run `wc -w` on it. If it
is at most 2,500 words, leave it unchanged and stop. If it is longer, do not
redo the research: shorten the existing map without removing the marker until
it is at most 2,500 words, then stop.

Explicitly search biohacker, psychonaut, nootropics, rationalist, quantified-
self, and 'rat' or research-chemical communities where relevant. Include other
adjacent experimental communities only when the search finds a concrete link.
Map their vocabulary, models, protocol habits, measurement practices, recurring
ideas, disagreements, failed experiments, and post-hoc storytelling. Keep this
as a terrain map rather than extracting every stack or turning it into advice.

Work on this pass yourself. Do not delegate, spawn subagents, or launch another
Mill, Spindle, Horizon, or agent workflow.

Evaluate these communities on their own terms while noticing copying,
confounding stacks, commercial influence, missing follow-up, and selective
reporting. Do not compare them with PubMed.

Bound the pass: aim for 8–12 useful sources across multiple communities, with
12 an absolute maximum rather than a quota, and stop at saturation. Never fill
inaccessible details from model memory. Keep the entire map at most 2,500 words:
no more than six broad experimental strains, six meaningful disagreements or
gaps, and a concise source index.

Write research/source-maps/{slug}/50-experimental-communities.md with:
1. Communities and search routes visited
2. Vocabulary and conceptual terrain
3. Recurring experimental strains of thought
4. Measurement habits, disagreements, failures, and negative space
5. Access failures and unanswered questions
6. Source index

Write only this file; create no other files.
Use local citation IDs [EX1], [EX2], and so on. Every substantive description
must point to a source. Record author/handle when public, community/site, title,
date, stable URL, access date, relevant context, and what it contributed.
Clearly label inference. Do not recommend, compare domains, select practices,
or design a test. Mark any source not opened directly as snippet-only, and
preserve one short verbatim excerpt from each source, no longer than 35 words.
One citation ID must identify one retrievable page, thread, or document; never
bundle several sources under one ID. Every ID used in the body must have
exactly one source-index entry, and every source-index entry must be cited at
least once in the body. The N in the marker counts source-index entries. End
the
file with `MAP COMPLETE — N sources used`, replacing N with the source count.
Then run `wc -w research/source-maps/{slug}/50-experimental-communities.md`. If
it exceeds 2,500 words, shorten the map without removing the marker and repeat
the check until the final file is at most 2,500 words.""" timeout=2400

    CC: """Map practitioner, household, and independent folk-web material around
this question:
{question}

Commission boundary: {boundaries}
Commission seeds: {seeds}

If research/source-maps/{slug}/60-practitioner-folk-web.md already exists and
its final nonblank line begins `MAP COMPLETE —`, run `wc -w` on it. If it is
at most 2,500 words, leave it unchanged and stop. If it is longer, do not redo
the research: shorten the existing map without removing the marker until it is
at most 2,500 words, then stop.

Search practicing herbalists, community educators, household remedy archives,
regional folk sources, newsletters, podcasts with transcripts or detailed show
notes, and independent sites that do not fit Reddit, academia, or storefronts.
Map named approaches, vocabulary, preparation traditions, recurring lines of
reasoning, disagreements, and practical knowledge. Preserve attributable
practice rather than collapsing everything into generic wellness content.

Work on this pass yourself. Do not delegate, spawn subagents, or launch another
Mill, Spindle, Horizon, or agent workflow.

Notice credential theater, copied material, undisclosed sales relationships,
universal-remedy language, missing failures, and cases where a source is
specific about when something does not fit. Do not use clinical uptake as the
default measure of value.

Bound the pass: aim for 8–12 useful sources across multiple independent voices,
with 12 an absolute maximum rather than a quota. Stop at saturation and never
invent inaccessible details. Keep the entire map at most 2,500 words: no more
than six broad lineages or strains, six meaningful disagreements or gaps, and
a concise source index.

Write research/source-maps/{slug}/60-practitioner-folk-web.md with:
1. Search routes and kinds of practitioners/sources visited
2. Vocabulary and conceptual terrain
3. Major practice lineages or strains of thought, kept broad
4. Disagreements, boundaries, failures, and negative space
5. Independence and commercial-entanglement notes
6. Access failures and unanswered questions
7. Source index

Write only this file; create no other files.
Use local citation IDs [PF1], [PF2], and so on. Every substantive description
must point to a source. Record author/practitioner, site or publication, title,
date, stable URL, access date, relevant context, commercial relationship when
visible, and what it contributed. Clearly label inference. Do not recommend,
compare domains, select practices, or design a test. Mark any source not opened
directly as snippet-only, and preserve one short verbatim excerpt from each
source, no longer than 35 words. One citation ID must identify one retrievable
page, episode, or document; never bundle several sources under one ID. Every ID
used in the body must have exactly one source-index entry, and every
source-index entry must be cited at least once in the body. The N in the marker
counts source-index entries. End the file with
`MAP COMPLETE — N sources used`, replacing N with the source count. Then run
`wc -w research/source-maps/{slug}/60-practitioner-folk-web.md`. If it exceeds
2,500 words, shorten the map without removing the marker and repeat the check
until the final file is at most 2,500 words.""" timeout=2400
